    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#VM">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><placeName type="ethnic"><supplied reason="lost">Ἀ</supplied>κραγαντῖνοι</placeName> τ<supplied reason="lost">ο͂ι</supplied> <persName type="divine"><name><supplied reason="lost">Ἀπό</supplied>
                    <lb n="2" break="no"/>λο̄νι</name></persName> <space extent="unknown" unit="character"/>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text after Arena 2002 (IGASMG II² 94).</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="lines 1–2">
                        <note>Arena prints the dialectal form Ἀπόλο̄νι and regularises it as Ἀπόλλωνι.</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="lines 1–2">
                        <note>Because the original width of the base cannot be established, the restoration remains uncertain. Dubois 1989 records the alternatives τ[ῶι Ἀπόλ]λωνι and τ[ὸ ἄγαλμα τῶι Ἀπόλ]λωνι; Arena 2002 adopts the former.</note>
                    </app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p>The Akragantines [dedicated this] to Apollo.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>Bousquet tentatively dated the dedication to the second quarter of the 5th century BCE and suggested a connection with the 
                    establishment of democracy at Akragas after the fall of the tyranny and the death of Theron in 472 BCE. De Waele, drawing on 
                    <ref target="https://scaife.perseus.org/reader/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0545.tlg002.perseus-grc2:2.33/">Aelian, Varia Historia 2.33</ref>, 
                    conjectured that the base may have supported the ivory statue of the river-god Akragas said to have 
                    been dedicated at Delphi by the Akragantines in commemoration of the advent of democracy in 471 BCE. Both historical 
                    identifications remain conjectural. Bousquet distinguished this smaller base from the other Akragantine dedication at Delphi, 
                    inv. 5098 (Dubois 182b).</p>
            </div>
